1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:06,560
Imagine a job where your boss takes three days of your week, unpaid.

2
00:00:07,017 --> 00:00:10,217
And if your daughter wants to get married, you pay him a fee.

3
00:00:10,683 --> 00:00:14,123
And when you die, he comes to your house and takes your best animal.

4
00:00:14,583 --> 00:00:17,783
Welcome to England, around the year thirteen hundred.

5
00:00:18,250 --> 00:00:21,210
You are a peasant, and this is your life.

6
00:00:21,650 --> 00:00:26,210
Maybe you picture a muddy person, working every single day, and dead by thirty.

7
00:00:26,650 --> 00:00:30,570
Some of that is true. A surprising amount of it is myth.

8
00:00:31,017 --> 00:00:34,137
Did the lord really get to spend the wedding night with your bride?

9
00:00:34,583 --> 00:00:38,023
Did peasants really work only a hundred and fifty days a year?

10
00:00:38,483 --> 00:00:44,003
Today, we'll live one full year in an English village, from harvest to harvest.

11
00:00:44,450 --> 00:00:48,050
We'll plough, sow, reap, pay, and get fined.

12
00:00:48,517 --> 00:00:54,437
And at the end, we'll meet the disaster that hit your village hardest, and strangely helped your grandchildren.

13
00:00:56,233 --> 00:00:59,593
First, you need a name. Let's call you Hugh.

14
00:01:00,050 --> 00:01:02,130
And Hugh is not completely made up.

15
00:01:02,583 --> 00:01:07,943
In 1279, King Edward the First sent officials around England to write down who held what.

16
00:01:08,383 --> 00:01:12,543
In a village called Alwalton, they wrote down a man named Hugh Miller.

17
00:01:12,983 --> 00:01:17,223
He was a villein, which means he held land from a lord, in return for work.

18
00:01:17,683 --> 00:01:22,163
His holding was a virgate, which in Alwalton meant twenty-five acres.

19
00:01:22,617 --> 00:01:26,057
That sounds like a nice big farm. It wasn't one piece.

20
00:01:26,517 --> 00:01:32,037
Your land came as narrow strips, scattered across the village fields, mixed in with your neighbors' strips.

21
00:01:32,483 --> 00:01:38,883
Hugh was in the middle of the pack. Alwalton had eighteen villeins with a full virgate, and five with half.

22
00:01:39,350 --> 00:01:43,430
Below them, a crowd of cottagers, with a hut and a tiny plot.

23
00:01:43,883 --> 00:01:50,283
England then had somewhere between four and a half and six million people. Historians still argue about it.

24
00:01:50,750 --> 00:01:54,270
Most of them lived in the countryside, in villages like this one.

25
00:01:54,717 --> 00:02:00,637
Above you sat the lord. In Alwalton, that was the abbot of Peterborough.

26
00:02:01,083 --> 00:02:07,163
His estate was called a manor. And on a manor, the lord is landlord, boss, and judge, all at once.

27
00:02:07,617 --> 00:02:12,177
Think of your village as a small company, where nobody asked you to sign the contract.

28
00:02:13,967 --> 00:02:18,527
Our year begins on the twenty-ninth of September, Michaelmas.

29
00:02:18,983 --> 00:02:23,863
The harvest is in. The lord's officials are adding up the year's accounts.

30
00:02:24,317 --> 00:02:29,197
The villagers choose a reeve, one of your own neighbors, to boss the farm work.

31
00:02:29,650 --> 00:02:33,490
Today, Hugh also owes a payment called tallage.

32
00:02:33,950 --> 00:02:36,430
The amount is whatever the abbot decides.

33
00:02:36,883 --> 00:02:40,403
Now you can start ploughing for winter wheat. In your own fields, right?

34
00:02:40,850 --> 00:02:44,130
Not so fast. First, you work for the lord.

35
00:02:44,583 --> 00:02:48,743
Hugh owes the abbot three days of work, every single week of the year.

36
00:02:49,183 --> 00:02:54,223
He gets exactly three weeks off, at Christmas, Easter, and Whitsun.

37
00:02:54,683 --> 00:02:58,203
In autumn it gets worse. He must send two men a day.

38
00:02:58,650 --> 00:03:02,170
On some manors, the lord at least feeds you during that work.

39
00:03:02,617 --> 00:03:06,937
So you plough your own strips on the other days, whenever the weather and the oxen agree.

40
00:03:07,383 --> 00:03:14,503
Ploughing is slow. Walter of Henley's handbook for estate managers says a team does three and a half roods a day.

41
00:03:14,950 --> 00:03:19,110
A rood is a quarter of an acre. So, less than one acre a day.

42
00:03:19,550 --> 00:03:23,550
Walter loves oxen. He says horses cost more to keep.

43
00:03:24,017 --> 00:03:29,617
Then comes the seed. Walter says you need at least two bushels of wheat for every acre.

44
00:03:30,083 --> 00:03:36,883
And he warns: if your harvest is only three times the seed, you gain nothing, unless grain sells well.

45
00:03:37,350 --> 00:03:42,630
So farming is a bet. You throw grain on the ground, and hope the weather pays you back.

46
00:03:43,083 --> 00:03:50,523
A prayer book made for a rich knight, around thirteen thirty, shows ploughing with oxen, harrowing, and the lord's mill.

47
00:03:50,983 --> 00:03:56,583
It's one of the best pictures we have of ordinary people from this time, drawn in the margins of a book for the elite.

48
00:03:58,400 --> 00:04:02,240
By December, the fields are quiet. Time to go home.

49
00:04:02,683 --> 00:04:06,283
Hugh's house is small, with one or two rooms.

50
00:04:06,750 --> 00:04:09,710
In some regions, the animals live in the same building.

51
00:04:10,150 --> 00:04:16,070
At Houndtor, on Dartmoor, archaeologists excavated a longhouse from the thirteenth century.

52
00:04:16,517 --> 00:04:19,877
People and cattle came in through the same door.

53
00:04:20,317 --> 00:04:26,477
The frame is wood. The walls are woven sticks plastered with mud. The roof is thatch.

54
00:04:26,917 --> 00:04:30,517
In the middle of the floor, there's a fire. It almost never goes out.

55
00:04:30,983 --> 00:04:35,463
There's no chimney. The smoke just drifts up and finds its way out through the roof.

56
00:04:35,917 --> 00:04:39,517
Your house is also your smokehouse. Congratulations.

57
00:04:39,983 --> 00:04:45,583
For a long time, historians thought houses like these fell apart within a generation.

58
00:04:46,050 --> 00:04:51,810
But later medieval houses survive in their thousands, so many were sturdier than we imagined.

59
00:04:52,250 --> 00:04:55,050
When the sun goes down, so does most of your day.

60
00:04:55,517 --> 00:04:59,197
At Christmas, Hugh finally gets his week off from the abbot's work.

61
00:04:59,650 --> 00:05:05,570
The church gives you more days off too. Sundays, and a long list of saints' days.

62
00:05:06,017 --> 00:05:11,617
In winter, you thresh the grain, repair your tools, and mend your fences. Nothing glamorous.

63
00:05:13,433 --> 00:05:18,473
Spring begins with a strange date: the twenty-fifth of March, Lady Day.

64
00:05:18,917 --> 00:05:24,517
In England, that was New Year's Day. The legal year started in March, not January.

65
00:05:24,983 --> 00:05:27,943
And it stayed that way until seventeen fifty-two.

66
00:05:28,383 --> 00:05:32,303
Now you sow the spring crops: barley, oats, and peas.

67
00:05:32,750 --> 00:05:35,390
Then comes Lent, forty days without meat.

68
00:05:35,850 --> 00:05:39,450
And here's a little secret of farming: spring is hungry.

69
00:05:39,917 --> 00:05:43,917
Last year's grain is running out. This year's grain isn't ripe yet.

70
00:05:44,383 --> 00:05:48,223
Cottagers feel it the hardest, with a tiny plot and no reserves.

71
00:05:48,683 --> 00:05:54,443
At the time, people believed a farthing a day, the price of a loaf, could keep a person alive.

72
00:05:54,883 --> 00:05:57,363
A farthing is a quarter of a penny.

73
00:05:57,817 --> 00:06:01,577
Then Easter arrives, and Hugh's gift to the abbot is on the list.

74
00:06:02,017 --> 00:06:05,457
Three hens, a rooster, and five eggs.

75
00:06:05,917 --> 00:06:08,317
Happy Easter, from your landlord.

76
00:06:10,133 --> 00:06:11,653
So, what do you eat?

77
00:06:12,117 --> 00:06:18,517
Mostly grain. Around thirteen hundred, up to eighty percent of a harvest worker's calories came from it.

78
00:06:18,983 --> 00:06:23,223
Grain shows up in three ways: bread, pottage, and ale.

79
00:06:23,683 --> 00:06:29,043
Bread is dark and heavy, often made from barley or rye, not fine white wheat.

80
00:06:29,483 --> 00:06:35,563
Pottage is a thick stew, boiled with grain, peas, and vegetables, and maybe a scrap of bacon.

81
00:06:36,017 --> 00:06:41,217
Ale is brewed from barley, and it spoils fast. So somebody is always brewing.

82
00:06:41,683 --> 00:06:48,963
Who? Usually women. Historian Judith Bennett found that women brewed and sold most of England's ale.

83
00:06:49,417 --> 00:06:53,417
Meat is rare. A fattened pig can give you bacon for the winter.

84
00:06:53,883 --> 00:07:01,803
In the thirteenth century, harvest workers were given much bread, some cheese, and only small amounts of ale, fish, and meat.

85
00:07:02,250 --> 00:07:04,970
And those workers ate better than most peasants.

86
00:07:05,417 --> 00:07:11,097
So next time you complain about your sandwich, think of Hugh's lunch: bread, with a side of bread.

87
00:07:12,900 --> 00:07:15,300
Then comes August, and the harvest.

88
00:07:15,750 --> 00:07:18,550
This is the moment that decides your whole year.

89
00:07:19,017 --> 00:07:23,737
Everybody helps. Wife, children, neighbors. All day, in the heat.

90
00:07:24,183 --> 00:07:29,303
The grain is cut by hand, with a sickle, bent over, row after row.

91
00:07:29,750 --> 00:07:33,670
And remember, the abbot gets two of your workers a day in autumn.

92
00:07:34,117 --> 00:07:37,477
His grain gets cut first. Yours waits for the weather.

93
00:07:37,917 --> 00:07:42,477
Then the sheaves are stacked, carried, threshed, and stored.

94
00:07:42,917 --> 00:07:48,997
And now, the big myth. "Medieval peasants worked only a hundred and fifty days a year."

95
00:07:49,450 --> 00:07:56,250
You've probably heard it. It spread from a popular book in the early nineteen-nineties, and it used the lowest numbers it could find.

96
00:07:56,717 --> 00:08:03,757
Economist Gregory Clark looked again, and found that medieval farm workers worked at least about two hundred and fifty days a year.

97
00:08:04,217 --> 00:08:10,457
Another study found that a farmer needed around three hundred days of work to feed a family before the Black Death.

98
00:08:10,917 --> 00:08:14,197
So no. You do not have half the year off.

99
00:08:14,650 --> 00:08:19,450
You get Sundays, a pile of saints' days, and three weeks free from the abbot.

100
00:08:19,917 --> 00:08:24,557
If the weather was kind, you'll eat this winter. If not, keep watching until the end.

101
00:08:26,367 --> 00:08:29,967
Every three or four weeks, the whole village has to show up for court.

102
00:08:30,417 --> 00:08:35,377
It's called the manor court. Think of it as a neighborhood meeting, where you can be fined.

103
00:08:35,817 --> 00:08:38,937
The fines are called amercements.

104
00:08:39,383 --> 00:08:42,503
And everything is written down, on parchment rolls.

105
00:08:42,950 --> 00:08:47,030
Some of these rolls survive. We can read what people actually got fined for.

106
00:08:47,483 --> 00:08:55,403
In Morden, near London, in October thirteen ninety-nine, the court's ale-tasters reported brewers who broke the assize.

107
00:08:55,850 --> 00:08:58,650
Their punishment was two pence per brew.

108
00:08:59,117 --> 00:09:03,917
Others were fined for breaking the lord's hedges, or for trespassing on a neighbor's land.

109
00:09:04,383 --> 00:09:10,783
In 1406, one man simply left the village. The court seized his empty land for the lord.

110
00:09:11,250 --> 00:09:18,370
Judith Bennett found that this ale fine worked more like a licence. Most brewers were fined, whether they cheated or not.

111
00:09:18,817 --> 00:09:21,617
So the "fine" was basically the price of a permit.

112
00:09:22,083 --> 00:09:26,643
And who judged all this? Mostly tenants. People like you.

113
00:09:27,083 --> 00:09:30,683
So when you complain about the lord's court, remember that your neighbors help run it.

114
00:09:31,150 --> 00:09:35,710
And if you hate it all, you can run. Some villeins fled to towns.

115
00:09:36,150 --> 00:09:41,830
A custom in many towns said that if a villein lived there for a year and a day, he became free.

116
00:09:42,283 --> 00:09:46,683
But he had to stay hidden, and not be dragged back, for that whole year.

117
00:09:48,500 --> 00:09:50,340
Let's add up Hugh's bills.

118
00:09:50,783 --> 00:09:54,543
To the lord: three days of work a week, tallage, hens, and eggs.

119
00:09:54,983 --> 00:09:57,463
If your daughter marries, you pay merchet.

120
00:09:57,917 --> 00:10:04,637
Now, about that myth. Yes, merchet was about marriage. No, it wasn't what the movies say.

121
00:10:05,083 --> 00:10:10,443
Historians have found no evidence that a "right of the first night" ever existed as a law in medieval Europe.

122
00:10:10,883 --> 00:10:15,763
Merchet was a fine, a payment to the lord, perhaps for losing a worker.

123
00:10:16,217 --> 00:10:19,017
Less movie, more tax form.

124
00:10:19,483 --> 00:10:22,523
When you die, your family owes the lord a heriot.

125
00:10:22,983 --> 00:10:24,903
That's usually your best animal.

126
00:10:25,350 --> 00:10:29,110
Then the church takes the second best. That's called a mortuary.

127
00:10:29,550 --> 00:10:33,310
Your family has just lost a person, and also two animals.

128
00:10:33,750 --> 00:10:38,310
And all year, the church takes a tenth of what you grow. That's the tithe.

129
00:10:38,750 --> 00:10:41,630
A tenth of your grain, paid in grain.

130
00:10:42,083 --> 00:10:45,683
Do the math. Half of Hugh's working week goes to the abbot.

131
00:10:46,150 --> 00:10:48,790
Plus a tenth of his harvest to the church.

132
00:10:49,250 --> 00:10:53,330
And whatever is left has to feed a family, and pay for seed.

133
00:10:55,133 --> 00:11:00,173
Hugh isn't doing this alone. Let's give him a wife. Her name is Alice.

134
00:11:00,617 --> 00:11:05,657
Alice works the fields, cooks, raises the children, and probably brews the ale.

135
00:11:06,117 --> 00:11:08,597
Everybody works, children too.

136
00:11:09,050 --> 00:11:14,250
A coroner's record mentions a ten-year-old boy hurt while chopping vegetables in a manor kitchen.

137
00:11:14,717 --> 00:11:21,197
Historian Jeremy Goldberg estimated that one in ten rural people over fourteen worked as a servant.

138
00:11:21,650 --> 00:11:27,570
Now, school. Can your son learn to read? Maybe. But not for free.

139
00:11:28,017 --> 00:11:31,377
A villein needed the lord's permission to send a child to school.

140
00:11:31,817 --> 00:11:39,657
In 1339, in Winslow, Richard Ponteys was fined three pence for sending his son Geoffrey to school without a licence.

141
00:11:40,117 --> 00:11:46,117
Three pence for a school without paperwork. Bureaucracy is older than you think.

142
00:11:46,583 --> 00:11:52,983
Some lords did give permission. At Norton, there were seventeen licences between 1300 and 1348.

143
00:11:53,450 --> 00:11:58,330
Why would a lord care? My guess: a schooled son is a worker who might leave.

144
00:12:00,133 --> 00:12:02,213
So, how long do you live?

145
00:12:02,650 --> 00:12:05,050
You've heard that people died at thirty.

146
00:12:05,517 --> 00:12:08,317
That's a statistic about birth, not about adults.

147
00:12:08,783 --> 00:12:14,463
Estimates for landholding families put life expectancy at birth around thirty-one.

148
00:12:14,917 --> 00:12:18,517
But so many children died young that they dragged the average down.

149
00:12:18,983 --> 00:12:23,303
Those who survived childhood often reached middle age, and beyond.

150
00:12:23,750 --> 00:12:30,230
Not that you'd be in good shape. No antibiotics, no vaccines, and not much help when you got sick.

151
00:12:30,683 --> 00:12:32,683
And what do you actually own?

152
00:12:33,150 --> 00:12:40,910
Here's a real list. In the thirteen-forties, a coroner's roll in Derbyshire listed the goods of a man named John Fox.

153
00:12:41,350 --> 00:12:46,870
A house and ten acres. A mare and her colt. Some cattle, sheep, and a pig.

154
00:12:47,317 --> 00:12:50,677
A brass pot. A pan. Two chests. And some oats.

155
00:12:51,117 --> 00:12:52,637
That's a man with land.

156
00:12:53,083 --> 00:12:56,443
Historians warn that lists like this leave things out.

157
00:12:56,883 --> 00:13:02,163
But the picture is clear. Your net worth is a house, some land, and a pot.

158
00:13:03,967 --> 00:13:05,887
So, how does Hugh's year end?

159
00:13:06,350 --> 00:13:12,510
If he's lucky, with enough grain. If he's unlucky, like in 1315, it starts with rain.

160
00:13:12,950 --> 00:13:17,830
In the spring of 1315, heavy rain began in England, and it kept falling.

161
00:13:18,283 --> 00:13:22,683
The grain couldn't ripen. The hay couldn't dry. Food prices shot up.

162
00:13:23,150 --> 00:13:28,110
The harvest failed in 1315, and again in 1316.

163
00:13:28,550 --> 00:13:32,870
People ate the seed grain. And a smaller harvest came next year.

164
00:13:33,317 --> 00:13:38,437
A few years later, a cattle plague killed more than half of England's cattle, by one estimate.

165
00:13:38,883 --> 00:13:43,043
Your oxen were your tractors. So that hurts twice.

166
00:13:43,483 --> 00:13:47,163
Then, in 1348, the Black Death arrives.

167
00:13:47,617 --> 00:13:51,457
Historians estimate that a third to a half of the English died.

168
00:13:51,917 --> 00:13:55,677
Villages like Alwalton would have lost many of their neighbors.

169
00:13:56,117 --> 00:14:02,037
And then comes the part nobody expects. Suddenly, there were too few workers and plenty of land.

170
00:14:02,483 --> 00:14:07,203
Lords needed workers. Workers wanted more pay. Wages rose.

171
00:14:07,650 --> 00:14:14,930
In 1351, Parliament passed the Statute of Labourers: wages must stay at pre-plague levels.

172
00:14:15,383 --> 00:14:16,823
It largely failed.

173
00:14:17,283 --> 00:14:21,763
Over time, lords turned work into cash rent, and villeinage faded.

174
00:14:22,217 --> 00:14:28,217
And in 1381, peasants marched on London, furious about a new poll tax.

175
00:14:28,683 --> 00:14:35,163
Here's my take. In 1300, the lord was not your biggest boss. The weather was.

176
00:14:35,617 --> 00:14:39,937
Too many people, too little land, and rain you couldn't argue with.

177
00:14:40,383 --> 00:14:45,823
The weak spot of the Middle Ages wasn't kings. It was the thin margin between a harvest and a famine.

178
00:14:46,283 --> 00:14:50,283
And it took a catastrophe to finally give people like Hugh some leverage.

179
00:14:50,750 --> 00:14:52,990
That's a grim kind of progress.

180
00:14:53,450 --> 00:14:56,570
Hugh never saw it. But his grandchildren might have.

181
00:14:57,017 --> 00:15:02,217
So next time you complain about taxes, remember Hugh, who gave half his working week to the abbot.

182
00:15:02,683 --> 00:15:08,843
If you enjoyed this year in the countryside, tell me in the comments: which life in history should we live next?
